1
00:00:03,237 --> 00:00:08,160
Deeming Sunday the best day for taking Mr. Wemmick’s Walworth sentiments, I devoted the

2
00:00:08,160 --> 00:00:14,116
next ensuing Sunday afternoon to a pilgrimage to the Castle. On arriving before the battlements,

3
00:00:14,116 --> 00:00:19,160
I found the Union Jack flying and the drawbridge up; but undeterred by this show of defiance

4
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:25,237
and resistance, I rang at the gate, and was admitted in a most pacific manner by the Aged.

5
00:00:25,237 --> 00:00:31,398
“My son, sir,” said the old man, after securing the drawbridge, “rather had it in his mind that

6
00:00:31,398 --> 00:00:36,917
you might happen to drop in, and he left word that he would soon be home from his afternoon’s

7
00:00:36,917 --> 00:00:45,680
walk. He is very regular in his walks, is my son. Very regular in everything, is my son.”

8
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:50,160
I nodded at the old gentleman as Wemmick himself might have nodded,

9
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:54,358
and we went in and sat down by the fireside.

10
00:00:54,358 --> 00:00:59,756
“You made acquaintance with my son, sir,” said the old man, in his chirping way,

11
00:00:59,758 --> 00:01:07,078
while he warmed his hands at the blaze, “at his office, I expect?” I nodded. “Hah! I have

12
00:01:07,078 --> 00:01:13,319
heerd that my son is a wonderful hand at his business, sir?” I nodded hard. “Yes;

13
00:01:13,319 --> 00:01:21,000
so they tell me. His business is the Law?” I nodded harder. “Which makes it more surprising

14
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:28,199
in my son,” said the old man, “for he was not brought up to the Law, but to the Wine-Coopering.”

15
00:01:28,199 --> 00:01:33,398
Curious to know how the old gentleman stood informed concerning the reputation of Mr. Jaggers,

16
00:01:33,398 --> 00:01:39,078
I roared that name at him. He threw me into the greatest confusion by laughing heartily

17
00:01:39,078 --> 00:01:44,078
and replying in a very sprightly manner, “No, to be sure; you’re right.” And to

18
00:01:44,078 --> 00:01:49,758
this hour I have not the faintest notion what he meant, or what joke he thought I had made.

19
00:01:49,758 --> 00:01:53,879
As I could not sit there nodding at him perpetually, without making some other

20
00:01:53,879 --> 00:01:59,800
attempt to interest him, I shouted at inquiry whether his own calling in life had been “the

21
00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:05,718
Wine-Coopering.” By dint of straining that term out of myself several times and tapping the old

22
00:02:05,718 --> 00:02:12,480
gentleman on the chest to associate it with him, I at last succeeded in making my meaning understood.

23
00:02:12,480 --> 00:02:19,756
“No,” said the old gentleman; “the warehousing, the warehousing. First, over yonder;” he appeared

24
00:02:19,758 --> 00:02:25,680
to mean up the chimney, but I believe he intended to refer me to Liverpool; “and then

25
00:02:25,680 --> 00:02:32,917
in the City of London here. However, having an infirmity—for I am hard of hearing, sir—”

26
00:02:32,917 --> 00:02:36,758
I expressed in pantomime the greatest astonishment.

27
00:02:36,758 --> 00:02:43,038
“—Yes, hard of hearing; having that infirmity coming upon me, my son he went into the Law,

28
00:02:43,038 --> 00:02:47,800
and he took charge of me, and he by little and little made out this elegant and

29
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:53,641
beautiful property. But returning to what you said, you know,” pursued the old man,

30
00:02:53,641 --> 00:02:59,758
again laughing heartily, “what I say is, No to be sure; you’re right.”

31
00:02:59,758 --> 00:03:03,800
I was modestly wondering whether my utmost ingenuity would have enabled me

32
00:03:03,800 --> 00:03:08,480
to say anything that would have amused him half as much as this imaginary pleasantry,

33
00:03:08,480 --> 00:03:12,358
when I was startled by a sudden click in the wall on one side of the chimney,

34
00:03:12,358 --> 00:03:18,718
and the ghostly tumbling open of a little wooden flap with “JOHN” upon it. The old man, following

35
00:03:18,718 --> 00:03:25,839
my eyes, cried with great triumph, “My son’s come home!” and we both went out to the drawbridge.

36
00:03:25,839 --> 00:03:30,519
It was worth any money to see Wemmick waving a salute to me from the other side of the moat,

37
00:03:30,519 --> 00:03:35,758
when we might have shaken hands across it with the greatest ease. The Aged was so delighted

38
00:03:35,758 --> 00:03:40,839
to work the drawbridge, that I made no offer to assist him, but stood quiet until Wemmick

39
00:03:40,839 --> 00:03:47,961
had come across, and had presented me to Miss Skiffins; a lady by whom he was accompanied.

40
00:03:47,961 --> 00:03:53,438
Miss Skiffins was of a wooden appearance, and was, like her escort, in the post-office branch

41
00:03:53,438 --> 00:03:58,800
of the service. She might have been some two or three years younger than Wemmick, and I judged

42
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:04,438
her to stand possessed of portable property. The cut of her dress from the waist upward,

43
00:04:04,438 --> 00:04:11,160
both before and behind, made her figure very like a boy’s kite; and I might have pronounced her gown

44
00:04:11,160 --> 00:04:18,116
a little too decidedly orange, and her gloves a little too intensely green. But she seemed to be

45
00:04:18,116 --> 00:04:24,038
a good sort of fellow, and showed a high regard for the Aged. I was not long in discovering that

46
00:04:24,038 --> 00:04:29,519
she was a frequent visitor at the Castle; for, on our going in, and my complimenting Wemmick on

47
00:04:29,519 --> 00:04:35,038
his ingenious contrivance for announcing himself to the Aged, he begged me to give my attention

48
00:04:35,038 --> 00:04:42,038
for a moment to the other side of the chimney, and disappeared. Presently another click came,

49
00:04:42,038 --> 00:04:48,116
and another little door tumbled open with “Miss Skiffins” on it; then Miss Skiffins shut up and

50
00:04:48,116 --> 00:04:54,160
John tumbled open; then Miss Skiffins and John both tumbled open together, and finally shut

51
00:04:54,160 --> 00:05:00,839
up together. On Wemmick’s return from working these mechanical appliances, I expressed the

52
00:05:00,839 --> 00:05:06,800
great admiration with which I regarded them, and he said, “Well, you know, they’re both pleasant

53
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:12,758
and useful to the Aged. And by George, sir, it’s a thing worth mentioning, that of all the people

54
00:05:12,758 --> 00:05:19,358
who come to this gate, the secret of those pulls is only known to the Aged, Miss Skiffins, and me!”

55
00:05:19,358 --> 00:05:26,756
“And Mr. Wemmick made them,” added Miss Skiffins, “with his own hands out of his own head.”

56
00:05:26,758 --> 00:05:31,758
While Miss Skiffins was taking off her bonnet (she retained her green gloves during the evening

57
00:05:31,758 --> 00:05:36,559
as an outward and visible sign that there was company), Wemmick invited me to take a

58
00:05:36,559 --> 00:05:42,800
walk with him round the property, and see how the island looked in wintertime. Thinking that

59
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:48,078
he did this to give me an opportunity of taking his Walworth sentiments, I seized the opportunity

60
00:05:48,078 --> 00:05:52,480
as soon as we were out of the Castle. Having thought of the matter with care,

61
00:05:52,480 --> 00:05:57,917
I approached my subject as if I had never hinted at it before. I informed Wemmick that I was

62
00:05:57,917 --> 00:06:03,438
anxious in behalf of Herbert Pocket, and I told him how we had first met, and how we had fought.

63
00:06:04,597 --> 00:06:09,961
I glanced at Herbert’s home, and at his character, and at his having no means but

64
00:06:09,961 --> 00:06:16,879
such as he was dependent on his father for; those, uncertain and unpunctual. I

65
00:06:16,879 --> 00:06:22,519
alluded to the advantages I had derived in my first rawness and ignorance from his society,

66
00:06:22,519 --> 00:06:27,758
and I confessed that I feared I had but ill repaid them, and that he might have done better without

67
00:06:27,758 --> 00:06:34,597
me and my expectations. Keeping Miss Havisham in the background at a great distance, I still

68
00:06:34,597 --> 00:06:39,839
hinted at the possibility of my having competed with him in his prospects, and at the certainty

69
00:06:39,839 --> 00:06:46,358
of his possessing a generous soul, and being far above any mean distrusts, retaliations, or

70
00:06:46,358 --> 00:06:53,961
designs. For all these reasons (I told Wemmick), and because he was my young companion and friend,

71
00:06:53,961 --> 00:07:00,438
and I had a great affection for him, I wished my own good fortune to reflect some rays upon him,

72
00:07:00,438 --> 00:07:05,879
and therefore I sought advice from Wemmick’s experience and knowledge of men and affairs, how

73
00:07:05,879 --> 00:07:12,116
I could best try with my resources to help Herbert to some present income,—say of a hundred a year,

74
00:07:12,116 --> 00:07:19,961
to keep him in good hope and heart,—and gradually to buy him on to some small partnership. I begged

75
00:07:19,961 --> 00:07:25,116
Wemmick, in conclusion, to understand that my help must always be rendered without Herbert’s

76
00:07:25,116 --> 00:07:30,839
knowledge or suspicion, and that there was no one else in the world with whom I could advise.

77
00:07:30,839 --> 00:07:37,237
I wound up by laying my hand upon his shoulder, and saying, “I can’t help confiding in you, though

78
00:07:37,237 --> 00:07:43,436
I know it must be troublesome to you; but that is your fault, in having ever brought me here.”

79
00:07:43,438 --> 00:07:48,718
Wemmick was silent for a little while, and then said with a kind of start,

80
00:07:48,718 --> 00:07:55,480
“Well you know, Mr. Pip, I must tell you one thing. This is devilish good of you.”

81
00:07:55,480 --> 00:07:58,519
“Say you’ll help me to be good then,” said I.

82
00:07:58,519 --> 00:08:03,398
“Ecod,” replied Wemmick, shaking his head, “that’s not my trade.”

83
00:08:03,398 --> 00:08:06,398
“Nor is this your trading-place,” said I.

84
00:08:06,398 --> 00:08:11,917
“You are right,” he returned. “You hit the nail on the head. Mr. Pip,

85
00:08:11,917 --> 00:08:17,116
I’ll put on my considering-cap, and I think all you want to do may be done by degrees.

86
00:08:17,116 --> 00:08:24,319
Skiffins (that’s her brother) is an accountant and agent. I’ll look him up and go to work for you.”

87
00:08:24,319 --> 00:08:27,000
“I thank you ten thousand times.”

88
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:33,398
“On the contrary,” said he, “I thank you, for though we are strictly in our private and personal

89
00:08:33,398 --> 00:08:40,838
capacity, still it may be mentioned that there are Newgate cobwebs about, and it brushes them away.”

90
00:08:40,839 --> 00:08:46,000
After a little further conversation to the same effect, we returned into the Castle where we found

91
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:53,078
Miss Skiffins preparing tea. The responsible duty of making the toast was delegated to the Aged,

92
00:08:53,078 --> 00:08:57,319
and that excellent old gentleman was so intent upon it that he seemed to me in

93
00:08:57,319 --> 00:09:02,917
some danger of melting his eyes. It was no nominal meal that we were going to make,

94
00:09:02,917 --> 00:09:09,879
but a vigorous reality. The Aged prepared such a hay-stack of buttered toast, that I

95
00:09:09,879 --> 00:09:14,917
could scarcely see him over it as it simmered on an iron stand hooked on to the top-bar;

96
00:09:14,917 --> 00:09:19,559
while Miss Skiffins brewed such a jorum of tea, that the pig in the back premises

97
00:09:19,559 --> 00:09:26,199
became strongly excited, and repeatedly expressed his desire to participate in the entertainment.

98
00:09:26,199 --> 00:09:31,678
The flag had been struck, and the gun had been fired, at the right moment of time,

99
00:09:31,680 --> 00:09:36,800
and I felt as snugly cut off from the rest of Walworth as if the moat were thirty feet wide by

100
00:09:36,800 --> 00:09:44,038
as many deep. Nothing disturbed the tranquillity of the Castle, but the occasional tumbling open of

101
00:09:44,038 --> 00:09:50,116
John and Miss Skiffins: which little doors were a prey to some spasmodic infirmity that made me

102
00:09:50,116 --> 00:09:55,917
sympathetically uncomfortable until I got used to it. I inferred from the methodical nature of Miss

103
00:09:55,917 --> 00:10:01,879
Skiffins’s arrangements that she made tea there every Sunday night; and I rather suspected that a

104
00:10:01,879 --> 00:10:07,398
classic brooch she wore, representing the profile of an undesirable female with a very straight nose

105
00:10:07,398 --> 00:10:13,557
and a very new moon, was a piece of portable property that had been given her by Wemmick.

106
00:10:13,559 --> 00:10:19,237
We ate the whole of the toast, and drank tea in proportion, and it was delightful

107
00:10:19,237 --> 00:10:25,718
to see how warm and greasy we all got after it. The Aged especially, might have passed

108
00:10:25,718 --> 00:10:33,116
for some clean old chief of a savage tribe, just oiled. After a short pause of repose,

109
00:10:33,800 --> 00:10:38,597
Miss Skiffins—in the absence of the little servant who, it seemed, retired to the bosom

110
00:10:38,597 --> 00:10:44,000
of her family on Sunday afternoons—washed up the tea-things, in a trifling lady-like

111
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:49,758
amateur manner that compromised none of us. Then, she put on her gloves again,

112
00:10:49,758 --> 00:10:57,438
and we drew round the fire, and Wemmick said, “Now, Aged Parent, tip us the paper.”

113
00:10:57,438 --> 00:11:03,199
Wemmick explained to me while the Aged got his spectacles out, that this was according to custom,

114
00:11:03,199 --> 00:11:08,718
and that it gave the old gentleman infinite satisfaction to read the news aloud. “I won’t

115
00:11:08,718 --> 00:11:15,758
offer an apology,” said Wemmick, “for he isn’t capable of many pleasures—are you, Aged P.?”

116
00:11:15,758 --> 00:11:20,961
“All right, John, all right,” returned the old man, seeing himself spoken to.

117
00:11:20,961 --> 00:11:25,000
“Only tip him a nod every now and then when he looks off his paper,” said Wemmick,

118
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:30,237
“and he’ll be as happy as a king. We are all attention, Aged One.”

119
00:11:30,237 --> 00:11:33,237
“All right, John, all right!” returned the cheerful old man,

120
00:11:33,237 --> 00:11:38,038
so busy and so pleased, that it really was quite charming.

121
00:11:38,038 --> 00:11:43,199
The Aged’s reading reminded me of the classes at Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt’s, with the pleasanter

122
00:11:43,199 --> 00:11:49,199
peculiarity that it seemed to come through a keyhole. As he wanted the candles close to him,

123
00:11:49,199 --> 00:11:53,718
and as he was always on the verge of putting either his head or the newspaper into them,

124
00:11:53,718 --> 00:11:59,917
he required as much watching as a powder-mill. But Wemmick was equally untiring and gentle in his

125
00:11:59,917 --> 00:12:08,199
vigilance, and the Aged read on, quite unconscious of his many rescues. Whenever he looked at us,

126
00:12:08,199 --> 00:12:14,519
we all expressed the greatest interest and amazement, and nodded until he resumed again.

127
00:12:14,519 --> 00:12:19,641
As Wemmick and Miss Skiffins sat side by side, and as I sat in a shadowy corner,

128
00:12:19,641 --> 00:12:25,839
I observed a slow and gradual elongation of Mr. Wemmick’s mouth, powerfully suggestive of his

129
00:12:25,839 --> 00:12:32,078
slowly and gradually stealing his arm round Miss Skiffins’s waist. In course of time I saw his hand

130
00:12:32,078 --> 00:12:37,078
appear on the other side of Miss Skiffins; but at that moment Miss Skiffins neatly stopped him with

131
00:12:37,078 --> 00:12:42,680
the green glove, unwound his arm again as if it were an article of dress, and with the greatest

132
00:12:42,680 --> 00:12:50,680
deliberation laid it on the table before her. Miss Skiffins’s composure while she did this was one

133
00:12:50,680 --> 00:12:55,800
of the most remarkable sights I have ever seen, and if I could have thought the act consistent

134
00:12:55,800 --> 00:13:02,160
with abstraction of mind, I should have deemed that Miss Skiffins performed it mechanically.

135
00:13:02,160 --> 00:13:07,199
By and by, I noticed Wemmick’s arm beginning to disappear again, and gradually fading out

136
00:13:07,199 --> 00:13:15,116
of view. Shortly afterwards, his mouth began to widen again. After an interval of suspense on

137
00:13:15,116 --> 00:13:20,319
my part that was quite enthralling and almost painful, I saw his hand appear on the other

138
00:13:20,319 --> 00:13:27,678
side of Miss Skiffins. Instantly, Miss Skiffins stopped it with the neatness of a placid boxer,

139
00:13:27,680 --> 00:13:34,358
took off that girdle or cestus as before, and laid it on the table. Taking the table to represent the

140
00:13:34,358 --> 00:13:40,436
path of virtue, I am justified in stating that during the whole time of the Aged’s reading,

141
00:13:40,438 --> 00:13:45,559
Wemmick’s arm was straying from the path of virtue and being recalled to it by Miss Skiffins.

142
00:13:45,559 --> 00:13:52,277
At last, the Aged read himself into a light slumber. This was the time for Wemmick to produce

143
00:13:52,277 --> 00:13:58,078
a little kettle, a tray of glasses, and a black bottle with a porcelain-topped cork, representing

144
00:13:58,078 --> 00:14:05,078
some clerical dignitary of a rubicund and social aspect. With the aid of these appliances we all

145
00:14:05,078 --> 00:14:12,199
had something warm to drink, including the Aged, who was soon awake again. Miss Skiffins mixed,

146
00:14:12,199 --> 00:14:17,718
and I observed that she and Wemmick drank out of one glass. Of course I knew better than to

147
00:14:17,718 --> 00:14:23,398
offer to see Miss Skiffins home, and under the circumstances I thought I had best go first;

148
00:14:23,398 --> 00:14:29,199
which I did, taking a cordial leave of the Aged, and having passed a pleasant evening.

149
00:14:29,199 --> 00:14:34,319
Before a week was out, I received a note from Wemmick, dated Walworth, stating that he hoped he

150
00:14:34,319 --> 00:14:39,639
had made some advance in that matter appertaining to our private and personal capacities, and that

151
00:14:39,641 --> 00:14:45,199
he would be glad if I could come and see him again upon it. So, I went out to Walworth again,

152
00:14:45,199 --> 00:14:50,961
and yet again, and yet again, and I saw him by appointment in the City several times,

153
00:14:50,961 --> 00:14:58,116
but never held any communication with him on the subject in or near Little Britain. The upshot was,

154
00:14:58,116 --> 00:15:03,078
that we found a worthy young merchant or shipping-broker, not long established in business,

155
00:15:03,078 --> 00:15:08,237
who wanted intelligent help, and who wanted capital, and who in due course of time and

156
00:15:08,237 --> 00:15:14,519
receipt would want a partner. Between him and me, secret articles were signed of which Herbert was

157
00:15:14,519 --> 00:15:20,519
the subject, and I paid him half of my five hundred pounds down, and engaged for sundry

158
00:15:20,519 --> 00:15:26,879
other payments: some, to fall due at certain dates out of my income: some, contingent on

159
00:15:26,879 --> 00:15:32,917
my coming into my property. Miss Skiffins’s brother conducted the negotiation. Wemmick

160
00:15:32,917 --> 00:15:39,718
pervaded it throughout, but never appeared in it. The whole business was so cleverly managed,

161
00:15:39,718 --> 00:15:44,758
that Herbert had not the least suspicion of my hand being in it. I never shall

162
00:15:44,758 --> 00:15:49,597
forget the radiant face with which he came home one afternoon, and told me,

163
00:15:49,597 --> 00:15:55,957
as a mighty piece of news, of his having fallen in with one Clarriker (the young merchant’s name),

164
00:15:55,957 --> 00:16:01,718
and of Clarriker’s having shown an extraordinary inclination towards him, and of his belief that

165
00:16:01,718 --> 00:16:09,718
the opening had come at last. Day by day as his hopes grew stronger and his face brighter, he must

166
00:16:09,718 --> 00:16:14,758
have thought me a more and more affectionate friend, for I had the greatest difficulty

167
00:16:14,758 --> 00:16:22,160
in restraining my tears of triumph when I saw him so happy. At length, the thing being done,

168
00:16:22,160 --> 00:16:26,957
and he having that day entered Clarriker’s House, and he having talked to me for a whole evening in

169
00:16:26,957 --> 00:16:33,199
a flush of pleasure and success, I did really cry in good earnest when I went to bed, to think that

170
00:16:33,199 --> 00:16:39,519
my expectations had done some good to somebody. A great event in my life, the turning point of my

171
00:16:39,519 --> 00:16:46,000
life, now opens on my view. But, before I proceed to narrate it, and before I pass on to all the

172
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:52,641
changes it involved, I must give one chapter to Estella. It is not much to give to the theme that

173
00:16:52,641 --> 00:16:55,000
so long filled my heart. Chapter 38.
